Network Intrusion Detection Write-Up
Kayla Teekaram
1. Data Preprocessing
The dataset arrived split across three CSV files (m_1.csv, m_2.csv, m_3.csv), each containing
network traffic logs with 80 feature columns and a Label column. After concatenating the files,
the following issues were identified and cleaned up in order:
• Duplicate header rows: Because the original large CSV was split into three files, some
rows contained the literal string 'Label' in the Label column. These were removed before
any numeric conversion.
• Non-numeric Timestamp column: The Timestamp column contained date/time strings
that could not be converted to numbers, so it was dropped entirely.
• Mixed-type feature columns: All remaining feature columns were cast to numeric using
pd.to_numeric with errors='coerce'. Values that could not be parsed became NaN and
were handled in the next step.
• Sentinel value -1: Certain columns used -1 as a placeholder for missing data rather than
NaN. These were replaced with NaN for consistent handling.
• Infinite values: Columns like flow bytes per second contained positive and negative
infinity from division by near-zero durations. These were replaced with NaN.
• Row-level NaN drop: After all replacements, any row still containing NaN was dropped
entirely.
• Zero-variance columns: Columns with a standard deviation of zero were removed since
they carry no information and can cause issues with StandardScaler.
An important finding emerged from the preprocessing audit: the DDOS attack-LOIC-UDP class,
which made up roughly 0.05% of the raw data, was completely removed during the NaN/infinity
cleanup. Because those records disproportionately contained missing and infinite values, every
single one was dropped. As a result, only 6 of the original 7 classes survived into the modeling
stage, and the DNN was trained and evaluated as a 6-class classifier.
Feature scaling was applied using StandardScaler, fit only on the training split and then applied
to the test split. This prevents data leakage. The scaler zero-centers each feature and normalizes
variance, which is essential for Neural Network stability. Both tasks used an 80/20 stratified
train/test split (random_state=42) to preserve class proportions in both sets.
