5. Class Imbalance — DDOS attack-LOIC-UDP
The DDOS attack-LOIC-UDP class made up approximately 0.05% of the raw dataset, around
100 records out of roughly 200,000 total. Because these records disproportionately contained
infinite flow-rate values and missing fields, every single one was removed during the
NaN/infinity cleanup step. The preprocessing audit cell in the notebook confirmed this explicitly
— the encoder learned only 6 classes, and LOIC-UDP was completely absent from both the
encoder and the label distribution after preprocessing.
This means the DNN's reported metrics — accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 — do not reflect
any performance on LOIC-UDP whatsoever. A deployed version of this model would be
completely blind to that attack type. The macro-averaged F1 of 0.87 also looks better than it
would have if LOIC-UDP had survived, since the hardest class to detect is missing from the
evaluation entirely.
For the 6 classes that did survive, compute_class_weight('balanced') was applied to the
CrossEntropyLoss to give rarer classes a stronger gradient signal during training. This helped
improve recall on classes like DoS-SlowHTTPTest (4.45% of data), though FTP-BruteForce still
showed lower recall at 0.41, suggesting it was frequently confused with other attack types.
6. AI Discussion & Verification
AI coding tools were used to help generate boilerplate code, particularly for the PyTorch
DataLoader setup, training loop structure, and seaborn heatmap formatting. All AI-generated
code was reviewed before use. One correction was needed.
When generating the DNN architecture, the AI produced a final output layer followed by
nn.Softmax(dim=1), which was then passed into nn.CrossEntropyLoss. This is incorrect.
PyTorch's CrossEntropyLoss already applies log-softmax internally before computing the
negative log-likelihood. Applying softmax before the loss function means the model computes
log(softmax(softmax(logits))), which double-applies the normalization, compresses all outputs
toward uniformity, and causes gradients to vanish or become NaN. The model would fail to learn
anything meaningful.
The AI-generated (incorrect) code looked like this:
# INCORRECT — softmax applied before CrossEntropyLoss
self.network = nn.Sequential(
 nn.Linear(128, 64), nn.ReLU(),
 nn.Linear(64, num_classes),
 nn.Softmax(dim=1) # <-- wrong
)
criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
The corrected version removes the Softmax and passes raw logits directly to the loss function:
# CORRECT — raw logits, CrossEntropyLoss handles softmax internally
self.network = nn.Sequential(
 nn.Linear(128, 64), nn.ReLU(),
 nn.Linear(64, num_classes) # raw logits only
)
criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=class_weights_tensor)
This was verified using the official PyTorch documentation, which states that CrossEntropyLoss
is equivalent to combining LogSoftmax and NLLLoss. The fix was confirmed by observing that
the training loss decreased smoothly from the first epoch with no NaN values in the loss curve.
